image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=4\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2} - 4.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} - 4 \\\\ v &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3},8).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,- 2 \\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le 2 \\sqrt{3},\\,u^{2} - 4\\le v\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/23/2369c3299b61f996c6203e1e45a7a62c74cf9e8f4691c2d0941bdeec0a5ae53c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[\\int_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[v]_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(8)-(u^{2} - 4)du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(12 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 12 u]_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &=1(((24 \\sqrt{3})+(- 8 \\sqrt{3}))-((- 24 \\sqrt{3})+(8 \\sqrt{3}))) \\\\ &=1(32 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\ &=32 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186560984 "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\hspace{0.222em}Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{7}{5}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186561027 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=4{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=-\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563656 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186563730 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186565439 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{0}^{r}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565440 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/35/350b2dc98a09957c17f1ab629e21021c30fcccd9443068c722fe7565f04dcbfe.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9={r}^{2}+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+9}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573262 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =(3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j})-(-4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{i})+(-5\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =7\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573264 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =⟨2,-1⟩-⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-(-1),-1-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573275 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{c}) & =2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+(-3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573294 "To compute $-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} & =-2\\cdot ⟨7,4⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,-8⟩+⟨9,11⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573298 "To compute $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =(5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j})+(-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{i})+(-3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
